HW reveiw Zen of python

Key principles:
Readability matters most. Code is read far more often than it is written.
Explicit is better than implicit. Make behavior clear rather than relying on hidden assumptions.
Simple is better than complex. Choose straightforward solutions whenever possible.
Complex is better than complicated. If complexity is necessary, keep it organized and understandable.
Flat is better than nested. Avoid excessive indentation and deeply layered logic.
Sparse is better than dense. Use whitespace and clear formatting to improve readability.
Errors should not pass silently. Handle exceptions intentionally instead of ignoring them.
There should be one obvious way to do something. Consistency makes code easier to learn and maintain.
Practicality beats purity. Real-world solutions are often more important than perfect theoretical designs.
Namespaces are valuable. Organize code to avoid naming conflicts and improve clarity.

One sentence takeaway
Write code for humans first and computers second: make it clear, simple, explicit, and maintainable.
